module

public import Mathlib.RingTheory.TensorProduct.Maps
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Descent.Modules.FaithfullyFlatEffectiveDescent
import StacksCore.Tactic.EgaSgaAttribute

/-!
# Faithfully flat descent for algebras

Let `R → A` be a faithfully flat ring map and let `B` be an `A`-algebra. A descent datum on `B`
is an isomorphism of `A ⊗[R] A`-algebras `τ : B ⊗[R] A ≅ A ⊗[R] B` satisfying the cocycle
condition on the triple tensor product. This file shows that such a datum is effective: the
`R`-subalgebra `C = {b : B | τ (b ⊗ 1) = 1 ⊗ b}` of invariants has the property that the
canonical map `A ⊗[R] C → B` is bijective and carries the canonical descent datum of the base
change of `C` to `τ`.

This is the algebra step in the proof of Stacks 0245, effective fpqc descent for affine
morphisms: there one reduces to a faithfully flat map of affine schemes `Spec A → Spec R` and a
descent datum on an affine `V = Spec B` over `Spec A`, and the only thing the module-theoretic
Stacks 023N does not already give is that the invariants are a *subalgebra* — which holds
because `τ` is a ring homomorphism — and that the resulting identification respects the descent
data.

## Main definitions

* `ModuleCat.restrictModuleAlgebraEquiv`, `ModuleCat.algebraSourceEquiv` and
  `ModuleCat.algebraTargetEquiv` compare the `R`-module `ModuleCat.restrictModule R A` of an
  `A`-algebra `B`, and the two tensor products formed from it, with `B`, `B ⊗[R] A` and
  `A ⊗[R] B` carrying their own `R`-algebra structures.
* `ModuleCat.algebraTransition` packages a transition isomorphism of algebras as the
  `A ⊗[R] A`-linear transition isomorphism that `ModuleCat.DescentData` asks for.
* `ModuleCat.descentDataOfAlgebra` is the resulting descent datum for modules, Stacks 023G.
* `ModuleCat.descentInvariants` is the `R`-subalgebra of invariants of a transition
  isomorphism.
* `ModuleCat.descentInvariantsEquiv` identifies it with the invariant submodule
  `ModuleCat.invariantSubmodule` of the underlying module descent datum.

## Main results

* `ModuleCat.algebraSourceEquiv_smul` and `ModuleCat.algebraTargetEquiv_smul` identify the two
  `A ⊗[R] A`-actions of `ModuleCat.DescentData` with multiplication by the image of
  `A ⊗[R] A`; this is what makes an isomorphism of `A ⊗[R] A`-algebras an `A ⊗[R] A`-linear
  map.
* `ModuleCat.transition_smul_descentInvariants`: the canonical map `A ⊗[R] C → B` is compatible
  with the descent data. Neither the cocycle condition nor faithful flatness is used.
* `ModuleCat.bijective_productMap_descentInvariants`: for a faithfully flat `R → A` the
  canonical map `A ⊗[R] C → B` is bijective.

## Implementation notes

`ModuleCat.DescentData R A` takes its underlying module in the form
`ModuleCat.restrictModule R A N` for `N : ModuleCat A`, whose `R`-module structure is
`Module.compHom` along `algebraMap R A`. For `N = ModuleCat.of A B` that structure is *not*
the one `Algebra.toModule` gives from `[Algebra R B] [IsScalarTower R A B]`: the two agree by
`IsScalarTower.algebraMap_smul`, but an `Algebra` carries its scalar multiplication as data, so
the agreement is a proposition and not a definitional equality. Consequently the two tensor
products `ModuleCat.restrictModule R A (ModuleCat.of A B) ⊗[R] A` and `B ⊗[R] A` are different
types, and the comparison is made explicit by `ModuleCat.algebraSourceEquiv` and
`ModuleCat.algebraTargetEquiv`, which are the identity on elements. The ring structure lives
only on the second, which is why the hypotheses of this file are stated there.

The condition that `τ` be an isomorphism of `A ⊗[R] A`-algebras is spelled as the equation
`τ (algebraMap A B a ⊗ b) = a ⊗ algebraMap A B b`: a ring homomorphism out of `B ⊗[R] A` is
`A ⊗[R] A`-linear exactly when it agrees with the structure morphisms on the image of
`A ⊗[R] A`, and that image is generated by the elements `algebraMap A B a ⊗ b`.

The cocycle condition is a hypothesis, stated in the form the `cocycle` field of
`ModuleCat.DescentData` consumes, i.e. for the transition produced by
`ModuleCat.algebraTransition`. It is used only through
`ModuleCat.DescentData.invariantsBaseChangeEquiv`.

